LXMF-rs (Reticulum transport and LXMF wire format)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Coyote depends on the `reticulum-rs-transport` and `lxmf-wire` crates from
LXMF-rs (https://github.com/FreeTAKTeam/LXMF-rs). LXMF-rs is dual-licensed:
its package metadata declares `license = "EPL-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later"`, and
its LICENSE file carries the Eclipse Public License 2.0 text followed by a
completed Secondary Licenses Notice offering the source under the "GNU
General Public License, version 2 or any later version".

Coyote relies on the GPL-2.0-or-later arm of that disjunction, which is the
only arm compatible with Coyote's AGPL-3.0-only license (GPL-2.0-or-later
permits GPL-3.0, which AGPL-3.0 section 13 allows to be combined with AGPL
work). The EPL-2.0 arm is not compatible with the GNU General Public License
family and is not relied upon here.

Two obligations of Coyote's own follow from relying on that arm, recorded
here so they are not lost. Neither is an assertion of wrongdoing upstream.

  - Distributing a work under a GPL arm means shipping a copy of that
    license. LXMF-rs ships the EPL-2.0 text and the Secondary Licenses
    Notice but no copy of the GNU General Public License, and neither does
    this repository yet.
  - Coyote's package metadata still declares `license = "AGPL-3.0-only"`,
    which no longer describes the combined work once these dependencies
    ship. The aggregate expression has not been settled.

Transitive dependencies. `reticulum-rs-transport` enables its default
`storage` feature, which pulls in `rusqlite` with upstream's `bundled`
setting and therefore compiles the SQLite C amalgamation into Coyote.
`rusqlite` and `libsqlite3-sys` are distributed under the MIT License,
Copyright (c) 2014 The rusqlite developers; the MIT permission notice is
reproduced in the LICENSE-MIT file. SQLite itself is in the public domain.

`reticulum-rs-transport` also pulls in `bzip2` and `bzip2-sys`, Copyright (c)
2014 Alex Crichton, distributed under "MIT OR Apache-2.0" and used here under
the MIT License, whose permission notice is reproduced in the LICENSE-MIT
file. `bzip2-sys` bundles and compiles libbzip2, "copyright (C) 1996-2019
Julian R Seward", distributed under a BSD-style license that requires
redistributions to retain its copyright notice and conditions; that notice
travels with the bundled source in the `bzip2-sys` crate.

